**Changes from v1.0:**

- **New §7.9 — Bartending (the first legit job).** A full new minigame and the first *scheduled-commitment* system. Three drink stations, carry-one-drink constraint, patience-timed customers, speed-based tips. Built on a reusable employment/scheduling spine (EmploymentSystem) that future scheduled obligations (community service, other jobs) will ride on. Shifts are scheduled 12-36h out at legal hours, surfaced in the Appointment Panel, with a clock-in grace window, strikes, and a two-strikes-fired rule driven by global flags + dialogue. This is the concrete first step of the long-discussed legit axis. See §7.9.
- **§6.6 — Customer roster overhaul.** The referral chain is now the organic growth mechanism: completing deals raises trust, crossing a trust threshold (45) refers a new customer one tier *above* the referrer (one-shot per customer, capped at tier 4). Per-tier quantity bands replace the old formula (1-3 g at tier 0 up to 7-14 g at tier 4), so order sizes scale with relationship depth. A roster cap (12) evicts the weakest contact (lowest tier, then lowest trust) on every intake, producing gradual upward drift. Street pickups remain the tier-0 bootstrap. See §6.6 and the updated §7.
- **Player-set appointment times.** Returning a call no longer auto-assigns a random time. The payphone callback now offers 3-5 future time slots at one spot; the player picks. The Appointment Panel shows alongside (redocked top-right). Implemented via a dedicated time-picker panel that owns the pause while open. See §7 loop step 3.
- **Vendor dialogue + owner-presence gating clarified.** Vendors trigger their NPC's normal contextual dialogue on interact (awaited before the panel opens) — vendor_id doubles as the npc_id / .dlg key, and a shared `DialogueRuntime.trigger(npc_id, display_name)` now centralizes context-build + line resolution for both NPCs and vendors. Owner-presence gating uses `NPCDirector.is_npc_present(vendor_id)` (schedule-driven room presence), not a physical proximity check. See §5 vendor system.
- **Street-deal / pager pricing reconciled.** RETAIL_MULTIPLIER for pager deals set to 1.0 (was 1.5 — the source of early over-earning). The risk premium moved to street dealing instead (STREET_MULTIPLIER 1.5): street sales pay more because they're the higher-risk, sell-to-strangers channel. Street dealing now expects to sell dime bags as the base unit. See §5 pricing.
- **New §23.5 — Gameplay progression ladder (housing).** The concrete long-arc spine committed: Tent → Apartment room 1 → rooms 1&2 → room 3 (1&2 combined) → 3rd-floor apartment → buy the apartment complex (roof access, weekly income) → buy a house on the right side of the tracks. This is the spend-sink that the income tracks feed and the eventual clean-cash gate attaches to.
- **§7.95 — Bottle collection & sale (planned, not built).** A trivial bottom-rung legit income vector that was never added: collect discarded bottles/cans from the world, sell for deposit value. Logged as a near-term easy add.
- **Future design captured (§25 NEW — Post-MVP Design Threads).** Two substantial threads from design discussion that aren't built but are load-bearing for the game's identity: the **Courthouse / adjudication system** (personal heat → court date → fine/community-service/jail, all denominated in lost time), and the **clean vs. dirty cash** economy (two-meter model: heat = witnessed-crime, scrutiny = does-your-money-make-sense; dirty spends freely in the informal economy, clean required at formal gates like rent/property/mail-order). Also the other planned legit jobs (delivery, panhandling) and fronts (lemonade stand → laundering firehose). See §25.

