## 5. Economy ✅ STAGE 5 COMPLETE

### Income sources (MVP)

| Source | Track | Risk | Setup Cost | Skill Ceiling | Time Cost | Day-fill role |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Street dealing (2-bit)** | Dealing | Medium-high | Low (oz fronted by Hank) | Low | Spot-anchored, time-of-day windows | **The opening engine** |
| Weed growing | Dealing | Low | Medium | Medium | Slow (multi-day cycles) | Background maintenance |
| **Pager dealing** | Dealing | Medium | Medium (pager from Felix) | Medium-high | Variable, schedule-disrupting | **The schedule engine** |
| Car looting | Criminal | Medium | Low (slim jim from Hank) | Low-medium | Fast (seconds per car) | Filler / opportunistic |
| Chop shop deliveries (late) | Criminal | High | Med (skills) | High | Medium (delivery walks) | Big-payoff session activity |
| **Bartending** | Legit | None (legal) | None (hired via dialogue) | Low | **4h scheduled blocks, hard commitment** | **Downtime-filler + scheduling tension** |
| Bottle collection (planned) | Legit | None | None | None | Opportunistic, trivial | Bottom-rung clean trickle |

The two *illegal* tracks (Dealing, Criminal) mirror each other structurally. Each has an XP spine (DealerExperience, CriminalExperience), each has tier-gated content, each unlocks tools/contacts/locations as it progresses. The player can lean into either, both, or alternate.

The **legit track** is deliberately not a third XP spine. Legit jobs are flat income with no tier ladder — their long-term value is laundering and clean-cash access (post-MVP, §25), not raw profit. Bartending is the first built example; it introduces the *scheduled-commitment* pattern (you can't tend plants, take a page, or meet a customer during a shift) that is the real point of the legit axis. See §7.9.

Within the dealing track there's now a clean progression: street-deal to passersby (high friction, high heat per deal) → graduate to pager dealing (named repeat customers, scheduled deals, better margins) → grow your own product (eliminates wholesale cost). The pager is the dividing line between hand-to-hand and relationship-mediated dealing.

### Wallet

Multi-pool by design. MVP only uses `"cash"`. The pool architecture supports future expansion to `"clean"` (banked/checked) for catalogue mail-order, plus laundering bookkeeping. All Wallet methods take an optional pool parameter so the expansion is additive.

Starting cash: $50. Weekly rent: $100. Raw bud base value: $15.

Hank's debt on the fronted ounce: $X owed by Sunday of Week 1 (tune in playtest — target a number reachable by ~half the street-dealing potential of that week). Late penalty: $Y added per day past deadline (target: enough to feel real but not unrecoverable from a single bad day).

### Expenses
- **Rent:** weekly, due Sunday (auto-deducted). 2 misses = eviction (MVP: logged signal only; future: shanty-town tent fallback)
- **Hank's debt:** week-1 fronted-ounce balance, escalating after deadline (see §7.5)
- **Food:** stamina maintenance — skip too long and tomorrow's stamina cap drops (deferred, post-MVP polish)
- **Supplies:** soil, seeds, lockpicks, baggies, calling-card minutes, etc.
- **Gifts:** social currency
- **Bribes / fines:** when caught
- **Equipment upgrades:** scale (from Felix), pager (from Felix), grow lamps, planter beds, tools, eventually a skateboard (see §24)

### Vendor system

One `VendorInteractable` node type supports both buying and selling via two multipliers:

- `buy_multiplier` — applied to ItemDef.base_value when player buys from vendor
- `sell_multiplier` — applied when player sells to vendor

Setting `sell_multiplier = 0` makes a vendor buy-only. The VendorPanel UI handles both directions transparently — click your inventory to stage sells, click their stock to stage buys, see live net, confirm.

**Owner-NPC gating.** A vendor declares its identity via `vendor_id: StringName` (which also matches the owner NPC's id). On interact, the vendor checks `NPCDirector.is_npc_present(vendor_id)` — schedule-driven room presence, *not* a physical-proximity check. If the owner's current schedule window doesn't place them in this room, the counter refuses with a notification ("X isn't here right now."). Vendors whose owner is always-scheduled-present (or vending-machine style) just always pass. This expresses "store closed when the shopkeeper isn't there" by reusing the schedule system as the single source of truth, with no duplicated schedule logic on the vendor. (Schedule presence is room-granular, not standing-at-the-counter — being scheduled into the room counts as attending. A stricter proximity check is available if a roaming-in-room NPC ever needs it.)

**Vendor dialogue on interact (v1.1).** Interacting with a staffed vendor first triggers the owner NPC's *normal contextual dialogue* — the same line they'd say if you talked to them anywhere else — and the vendor panel only opens once that dialogue finishes (the interact handler awaits `DialogueRuntime.dialogue_ended`). `vendor_id` doubles as the npc_id / `.dlg` key, so there's one Oliver dialogue file, not a vendor-specific one. Both NPC interaction and vendor interaction route through a shared `DialogueRuntime.trigger(npc_id, display_name)` that builds the dialogue context (weekday/timeofday/location) and resolves the best-matching line — context logic lives in exactly one place. If there's no matching line for the current context, the panel opens immediately. This keeps vendors as character touchpoints (they're at their stations most of the game) rather than silent shop UIs, and lets flags be set/cleared through the existing dialogue parser — the mechanism intended for, e.g., Hank's wholesale-customer unlock.

**The three named vendors:**

| Vendor | Role | Buys | Sells | vendor_id |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Hank** | Contraband wholesaler + criminal-track contact | Raw bud, stolen goods, electronics, anything no-questions | Slim jim (when CRIM tier 1), electronics kit (when CRIM tier 2), wholesale product when player is short | `hank` |
| **Felix** | Pawn shop. Semi-legal, refuses obvious contraband. Equipment-availability gate. | Most non-contraband items at fair value (he knows what things are worth) | **Pager** (the dealing-track unlock), **scale** (eliminates eyeball tax), skateboard, used tools, eventually bike/rollerblades. Stock is curated — Felix's inventory is the lever for staging equipment unlocks. | `felix` |
| **Oliver** | Hardware store. Everything legit. | Doesn't buy player items (`sell_multiplier = 0`) | Pots, soil, seeds, watering cans — all growing supplies. | `oliver` |

The three are mirror positions on the same moral question: Hank profits from the neighborhood's decline, Oliver endures it with dignity, Felix occupies the slippery legal middle. This is intentional. Vendor choice is also character interaction.

**Tier-gated stock.** Vendors support `gated_stock` / `gated_stock_counts` / `gated_stock_min_tier` parallel arrays alongside `initial_stock` — items that only appear once `DealerExperience.current_tier()` reaches their `min_tier`. The shelf rebuilds on day-roll, week-roll, *and* on `DealerExperience.tier_unlocked` (so a newly-unlocked item appears immediately rather than waiting for the next morning). There's also a weekly-rotating pool (`rotating_pool` / `rotating_pick_count`) for variety. (v1.1 fix: tier-gated items weren't surfacing because nothing rebuilt the shelf on tier-up, and a duplicated `_ready` rehydration block was cleaned up.)

**Pricing example (reconciled in v1.1):**
- Raw bud wholesale to Hank: 0.6× base
- **Pager (relationship) deal: 1.0× base** — flat base value. The pager channel is the *safer, lower-margin* one; you trade a cut for reliability, no eyeball tax, no cop heat. (Was 1.5× — that was the source of early over-earning.)
- **Street deal: 1.5× base** (STREET_MULTIPLIER) — the risk premium lives here. Selling to strangers on a corner is the high-risk, high-touch channel, so it pays more per unit. Street dealing is expected to move **dime bags** as the base unit, not loose grams long-term.
- Street bags are still subject to eyeball tax until a scale is bought (§7.5).

The inversion is intentional: cultivating pager relationships rewards you with a *steadier, safer* channel even though the per-unit cut is lower; street dealing pays more but carries the danger. Pricing is keyed off each item's `base_value` in its `.tres`, so editing one number propagates.

### Pawn shop curation (intentional gate)

Felix's stock is the primary lever for staging equipment unlocks across the game's progression. Pre-pager (week 1), Felix's visible stock is limited: scale, baggies, calling cards, a watering can or two. Pager appears once player has paid Hank back (event flag triggers stock update). Skateboard appears later. Bikes / rollerblades later still. This makes Felix a recurring destination, gives equipment unlocks a moment of "he didn't have this last time," and routes all gear progression through one NPC the player has a relationship with.

Mechanically, this is just per-NPC stock-rotation logic gated on flags/tiers — same vendor scene, dynamic stock contents.

### Dirty vs. clean cash

Architected via the multi-pool wallet but inert for MVP. **Full design now captured in §25.3** — two-meter trouble model, dirty-spends-informal / clean-required-at-formal-gates, laundering coupled to grow expansion. The catalogue system, when it ships, will use a `"clean"` pool. Money laundering loop is post-MVP and gated on stabilizing the economy first.

