## 6.6. Customer Roster & Referral Chain ✅ COMPLETE (overhauled v1.1)

How the customer list grows, scales, and self-prunes. This is the organic-growth engine behind pager dealing — it replaces the old "waves of tier-0 customers on DEX tier-up" model with a relationship-driven chain.

### Acquisition: two sources

- **Street pickups (bootstrap).** Completing a street deal has a small chance (~3%) to add a tier-0 customer to the roster, once the pager is active. This is how you get your *first* customers — the bottom rung. Anonymous, always tier 0.
- **Referrals (the chain).** Each customer can refer exactly one new customer, once, when their trust crosses a threshold. The referred customer enters at **referrer.tier + 1** (clamped to a max tier). The new customer then carries the chain forward when *their* own trust crosses the threshold. This is the escalation path — a single bootstrapped tier-0 contact can seed a chain that climbs T0 → T1 → T2 → T3 → T4.

### Referral mechanics

- Trust rises +5 per completed deal (caps at 100), drops on flakes.
- When a completed deal pushes a customer's trust to **45+** and they haven't referred before, they refer one new customer at `min(tier + 1, MAX_REFERRAL_TIER)` and flip a one-shot `has_referred` flag (persisted in save).
- `MAX_REFERRAL_TIER = 4`. Tier-4 customers refer tier-4 customers (the chain plateaus rather than dying — each tier-4 still refers exactly one more tier-4). Beyond tier 4 is the wholesale track (oz-based, gated through Hank — the long-discussed next escalation; see §25).
- The referral fires from `MeetingManager.mark_completed` right after the trust bump, so the deal that crosses the threshold is the one that triggers the referral. A notification surfaces it.

### Quantity bands (per tier)

Order sizes are tier-derived, so deeper chains ask for more. Explicit per-tier table (replaced the old `1 + tier` formula):

- T0: 1-3 g
- T1: 2-5 g
- T2: 3-8 g
- T3: 5-11 g
- T4: 7-14 g

The actual rolled quantity is still gated by **trust** (a fresh, low-trust customer asks near their floor; the band opens toward the max as trust builds). So a high-tier referral starts asking meaningful amounts immediately (its *floor* is high) and grows from there. This fixed the "everyone asks for 1-2 g forever" problem — the old anonymous tier-0 referrals never scaled.

### Roster cap & self-pruning

- `MAX_ROSTER_SIZE = 12`. Enforced inside `_add_customer`, so every intake path (street pickups, referrals, any future source) is capped automatically.
- When an add exceeds the cap, the **weakest** customer is evicted: lowest tier first, then lowest trust, then fewest deals, then id (deterministic). A `customer_removed` signal fires.
- This is self-correcting: a fresh tier-0 pickup (trust 0) dropped into a full roster of established contacts will itself be the weakest and get evicted — the add cleanly no-ops, no special-casing needed.
- Net effect: the roster drifts *upward* over time. Stronger contacts displace weaker ones, so the 1-2 g crowd gets shed by attrition as the chain deepens. This same mechanism is what will let wholesale (tier-5) customers eventually push out the last grams-tier stragglers.

### Customer data model

Each customer carries: `tier` (gate + band), `affinity`, `trust`, `times_dealt`, `times_flaked`, `quantity_min`/`quantity_max`, `has_referred`, `blacklisted`. Repeat flakers still auto-blacklist. (Blacklisting is a real flag but currently has minimal downstream behavior — flagged as not-yet-a-full-mechanic.)

### Open knobs

- Trust threshold (45), max tier (4), roster cap (12), per-tier bands — all first-pass numbers, expect playtest tuning.
- Eviction protection for customers with an active pending page or scheduled meeting is *not* yet implemented — a customer could be evicted mid-obligation (handled gracefully by null-guards downstream, but worth revisiting).

