## 7.5. Two-Bit Street Dealing ✅ STAGE 7 COMPLETE

**The on-ramp loop.** Fills what playtesting revealed as a vacuum in the opening hour: not enough progression pressure, no inciting incident, the pager loop assumed product the player didn't yet have. Street dealing is the gameplay layer between "wake up in apartment" and "be a real dealer with repeat customers."

### Inciting incident: Hank's fronted ounce

On the player's first visit to Hank, his vendor panel offers FRONT mode (see §5 vendors). Player takes an ounce of weed on credit. A debt is recorded against `&"hank"` in DebtSystem; interest is 10% per rolling 7 days. Once owed, all subsequent vendor visits to Hank lock to DEBT mode — no shopping until debt cleared. Full-payment only; partial payment is a deferred enhancement.

This is the **week-1 timer.** Rent is week-2's problem. The deadline is felt but recoverable from a couple of bad days.

### Dealing spots

Outdoor scenes contain `DealSpot` markers (same spawn-pattern as MeetSpot, Waypoint — see §17). Each spot exposes inspector knobs that define its character:

- **spot_id** — used by SpotHeatTracker for cop spawn scaling
- **area_id** — heat scope for refusal/witness events  
- **spawn_interval_minutes** — base game-minutes between foot-traffic spawns
- **archetypes** + **archetype_weights** — what kinds of people walk this spot
- **cop_check_interval_minutes** — how often the cop spawn check rolls

A single time-of-day modifier multiplies spawn interval globally (evening 0.75×, day 1.0×, late night 1.4×, early morning 2.0×). Spots otherwise express their identity through their inspector config — author once per spot.

### The minigame model

Posting up is a scene change. Player E's a DealSpot → all bud is banked into a session counter → `RoomManager.change_room` to the dedicated `street_deal_minigame.tscn`. The real player is preserved in a frozen state via `input_locked`; the minigame scene has its own fixed camera and spawn markers off-screen left/right. NPCs walk straight across the screen.

On exit (player presses "Leave," or bud hits zero, or bust): leftover bud + earnings deposit back, return to the outdoor scene at the spot position. Time and stamina pass naturally during the session — posting up burns hours of the day.

**Idle-skip speed multiplier:** when no live customers exist on screen, `TimeSystem.push_speed(5.0)` engages — the world fast-forwards. A customer spawns → pop, normal speed. Walks off → push again. Empty-street tedium collapses to ~3-4 real-seconds per customer at a spot with ~30 game-minute base intervals; the player visibly sees the clock jump between encounters.

### Customer archetypes

Three archetypes ship at MVP, each a `CustomerArchetype` resource (`.tres`) with display name, sprite_frames pool, and a min/max purchase-chance range:

| Archetype | Purchase range | Sprite cue |
|---|---|---|
| **Pothead** | 70-100% | Casual / scruffy |
| **Normal** | 40-60% | Plain pedestrian |
| **Business Man** | 0-30% | Briefcase, suit |

Each spawn picks an archetype by spot-defined weight, rolls a willingness from the archetype's range, then rolls accept/refuse against that willingness. Sprite identity is the player's primary read; visual variety within an archetype comes from the per-archetype sprite_frames pool.

The Narc archetype from the previous design is not yet implemented. Refusals raise area heat uniformly 0-5 points (HeatSystem), regardless of archetype — refining heat consequences per archetype is a deferred polish item.

### Player-side reveal (DEX-gated)

An always-visible overhead label appears above customers once DEX ≥ tier 1. Reveal scales with DEX:

| DEX tier | Overhead label |
|---|---|
| 0 | (hidden) |
| 1 | `~<jittered range>%` — bounds wobble ±10% per spawn |
| 2 | `~<archetype min>-<max>%` — stable archetype range |
| 3 | `<rolled ±15>%` — fuzzy band around true value |
| 4 | `<rolled>%` — exact |

The walk-across is the decision window. Higher DEX lets the player commit or skip from across the screen with confidence.

### Offer flow (deal as crime)

On player interact:
1. If unwilling: instant refusal, +0-5 heat to area, customer continues off-screen
2. If willing: customer stops moving, 2-second progress bar fills, `CrimeSystem.begin_crime(&"weed_deal", ...)` registers an active crime for the window
3. If player walks too far during the action: action cancels, crime ends with `Outcome.CANCELLED`, no sale
4. If a cop walks into FOV during the window: WitnessComponent witnesses the crime, CrimeSystem fires `crime_witnessed`, minigame handles bust
5. Completion: +$10 per gram (1-2g per accept), DEX XP +1 per gram, eyeball tax accumulates, 3% chance per accept of seeding a new pager customer (only if pager is active)

The progress-bar window is the bust window. Posting up safely means watching the street before pressing E on a willing customer.

### Bust mechanic

Cops spawn into the minigame scene as actual `CopNPC` instances (same scene used by PoliceSystem for patrols), unregistered from PoliceSystem on spawn so they don't enter pursuit independently — they patrol straight across as visual hazards. Their WitnessComponents remain active for the bust check.

Cop spawn check fires every `cop_check_interval_minutes` game-minutes. Spawn chance scales with `SpotHeatTracker.recent_deal_count(spot_id)` over a rolling 3-day window: 8% per recorded deal, capped at 60%. First deal at a fresh spot is always safe — `recent_deal_count == 0` short-circuits the roll.

A bust during a willing-progress deal: all session bud is lost, session ends immediately. The deal counter still increments. Walking off the spot to escape mid-session is acceptable; cops won't pursue across the scene boundary.

### Eyeball tax

Without a scale, every successful sale wastes a uniform 0-70% of the grams sold. Accumulates as a float across the session; at session end, the rounded total is deducted from leftover bud. Notification fires once at exit ("Shorted yourself Xg").

Scale upgrade and pre-packaged baggies remain as designed in the prior revision of this section, deferred to post-MVP.

### Per-spot heat tracking

`SpotHeatTracker` autoload tracks per-spot completed deals per game-day, with automatic rollover at `TimeSystem.day_rolled`. Days outside the 3-day window are pruned on rollover. Spots cool naturally as long as the player rotates.

### Implemented systems

- **DealSpot.tscn / .gd** — outdoor interactable. Wraps an Interactable; reads player's raw_bud, deducts it all into session, fires `StreetDealSession.begin_session()`
- **StreetDealSession** autoload — orchestrates the cross-scene minigame. Owns: return room/position, bud bank, spot config (archetypes, intervals)
- **StreetDealMinigame.tscn / .gd** — the puppet scene. Owns: live customers, live cops, spawn timers (driven by game-time, not real time), idle-skip speed multiplier, end-of-session settlement
- **StreetCustomer.tscn / .gd** — walks straight across, optionally stops on E for progress-bar action. Overhead `InfoLabel` shows tier-gated archetype info
- **CustomerArchetype.gd** — Resource. archetype_id, display_name, sprite_frames_pool, purchase_chance_min/max
- **SpotHeatTracker** autoload — per-spot, per-day deal counter with 3-day rolling window
- **DebtSystem** autoload — tracks per-creditor debt with 7-day interest rolling cadence

### Architecture notes

- Cop patrol cops in the minigame are unregistered from PoliceSystem in `_spawn_cop`. Their WitnessComponent stays active so CrimeSystem can witness the deal crime, but PoliceSystem treats them as if they don't exist (no top-up logic touches them, they free themselves on patrol completion).
- `CrimeSystem.begin_crime(&"weed_deal", ...)` is the bridge — same crime type used by pager deal handoffs (§7), so the heat surface treats street and pager deals uniformly when witnessed.
- The minigame inherits `TimeSystem.push_speed`'s pause/resume cleanly: pause() during shadow-inventory UIs (like vendor panel) coexists with speed-up because is_running() short-circuits during pause regardless of multiplier.

### Tuning targets (for playtest)

- Average week-1 dealing income: enough to pay Hank back AND have $30-50 left over for a scale and maybe a calling card, but NOT enough to also buy a pager
- Pager comes online: week 2, after Hank-payment event
- First bust: roughly day 3-4 if player is grinding one spot
- First scale purchase: end of week 1

### Open design questions

- Archetype heat differentiation — should a business_man refusal heat more than a pothead refusal? Currently uniform 0-5
- Bust apprehension flow — currently "Busted, lose product, session ends." Future: hospital flow, fine, criminal record bumps that gate certain dialogue
- Scale upgrade implementation surface — packaging interaction location, baggie item flow
