## 7.8. Collapse System ✅ COMPLETE

Three failure conditions route through one `CollapseSystem` autoload:

| Cause | Trigger | Penalty | Wake |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Exhaustion** | Stamina hits 0 (mid-day) | 10% cash | Tomorrow 16:00, apartment exterior, 50% stamina |
| **Hunger** | Hunger at 0 for 30+ game-min | 20% cash + all drugs | Tomorrow 16:00, apartment exterior, full bars |
| **Thirst** | Thirst at 0 for 30+ game-min | 20% cash + all drugs | Tomorrow 16:00, apartment exterior, full bars |

Exhaustion narratively: "Erik found you collapsed." Hospital narratively: "Woke up at the hospital." Both currently land at the apartment exterior — the actual hospital scene is deferred.

### Flow

1. Trigger fires (`spend_stamina` calls `CollapseSystem.trigger_exhaustion()`, or HungerSystem/ThirstSystem emit `collapsed`)
2. Penalties applied immediately (cash deducted, drugs confiscated from inventory)
3. `TimeSkipSystem.skip_to(next_wake_minute(), {kind: "collapse"})` runs — fade out, advance time, listeners fire while screen black
4. `CollapseSystem._on_time_skipped` (listening for `kind == "collapse"`) places player at wake anchor, restores stats per cause
5. Fade back in, notification with what was lost

### Architecture notes

- Wake location resolved via `apartment_exterior_anchor` group — any Node2D in that group in `ext_central.tscn` becomes the wake position. Fallback to `(0,0)` if missing.
- `DRUG_ITEM_IDS` is a constant array on CollapseSystem. Currently `[&"weed_buds"]`. Extends naturally as drug variety grows. Could promote to a category check (`item.category == DRUG`) once the ItemDef enum has that distinction.
- `_pending_cause` field guards against re-entry — if hunger AND thirst both hit zero in the same minute tick, only the first collapse runs; the second is no-op until the cause clears.
- Old `_force_sleep` at 6AM in player.gd remains as a safety net for the "made it to dawn with stamina intact" case.

### Future

- Real hospital scene + bill + DebtSystem hookup (already-designed bridge: can't afford bill → debt to `&"hospital"` creditor with same interest mechanic)
- Cause-specific wake animations (Erik finding you vs. waking in a hospital bed)
- Exhaustion wake should probably restore hunger/thirst to ~60% so player doesn't immediately collapse again


## 7.9. Bartending — First Legit Job ✅ COMPLETE (minigame + scheduling spine)

The first legit job, and the project's first **scheduled-commitment** system. Built this session end-to-end. Two layers, deliberately separated:

1. **The bartending minigame** — a self-contained scene controller (twin of the street-deal minigame): customers, drink stations, tips. Bartending-specific.
2. **The employment/scheduling spine** (`EmploymentSystem` autoload) — schedules shifts, tracks attendance, strikes, firing, pay. *Reusable* — community service (§25 courthouse) and any future scheduled job ride on this same spine. It knows nothing about drinks; it knows "it's shift time, did the player show up, launch the job scene, record the result."

This split is load-bearing: the scheduling layer is the spine, the minigame is one thing that can fill a shift block.

### Why bartending (design rationale)

The dealing loop has structural downtime (set an appointment hours out, then wait). The first instinct was a delivery job (cheap to build, reuses meetspots/timers) — but delivery is the *same verb* as dealing (route → interact → timer) and would deepen tedium rather than relieve it. Bartending is a genuinely new verb, and more importantly it's a **fixed commitment** that collides with the game's real core loop (scheduling: customers, plants, appointments). A 4-hour shift isn't 4 hours of content — it's 4 hours you *can't* tend plants or take a page. The minigame is almost incidental; the commitment is the feature. Bartending is "more game"; delivery would have been "more dealing." (Delivery and panhandling are still planned, gated behind map work and ambient-NPC work respectively — see §25.)

### The minigame

- **Bar scene** with a counter, three drink stations at different ends/middle (Beer / Whiskey / Cocktail), and a row of counter-slot `Marker2D`s where customers stand (slot count caps concurrent customers).
- **Customers** spawn at free slots on a timer, display a requested-drink icon, and run a **patience timer**. Ignored too long → storm out. Served correctly → leave happy. (`bar_customer.gd`, modeled on `street_customer.gd`.)
- **Drink stations** — one `drink_station.gd` script, three instances, each with an exported `drink_type` (clean id token: `beer`/`whiskey`/`cocktail`) and `prep_time` (different per drink — cocktail slowest, the stressful order). Interact → progress bar → produces a drink. Player is locked during prep (scoped lock, just for the progress bar — released on finish or scene-exit).
- **Carry one drink at a time** — the core constraint. The held drink is tracked on the minigame controller (not the player inventory, not a real item). A station refuses to prep while you're already carrying. This is what turns the bar into a routing puzzle: stations far apart + one-at-a-time = "serve the impatient one or the closer one?"
- **Delivery** — each customer has an `Interactable`; walk up holding their drink, press E → served, tip scaled by patience remaining. Wrong drink → rejected (keep holding). A drop/dump action clears a wrong/unwanted drink.
- **Tips** accrue as float across the shift, rounded up and deposited to the wallet once at shift end (single transaction + summary notification).

### Interaction arbitration (the clean solution)

Stations, customers, and the held-drink state all compete in `InteractionManager`. Rather than tune priorities, eligibility is encoded via `can_interact()`:
- A **station** is eligible only when **empty-handed** (and not mid-prep).
- A **customer** is eligible only when **carrying their exact drink**.

So holding a whiskey, standing between the whiskey station and a whiskey-wanting customer: the station filters itself out (not empty-handed), only the customer remains. Picking up / dropping a drink calls `InteractionManager.notify_player_state_changed()` to re-arbitrate (eligibility changes without a zone enter/exit). Priority only breaks genuine same-eligibility ties (customers > stations). This encodes the actual rule — "you can only do what makes sense for your current hand" — instead of hoping numbers sort it out.

### Time acceleration during shifts

A 4-hour shift would be tedious real-time waiting. The shift clock rides game-time (`TimeSystem.push_speed`/`pop_speed`, same stack the street game uses for idle-skip), so the clock — and the whole world — ticks faster during a shift. Critically, **prep and patience timers run on real `delta`**, so they're unaffected: drinks still take the same real seconds, customers wait the same real seconds, only the corner clock runs fast. `SHIFT_TIME_SCALE` (~2.5-3.0) is the pacing dial; the 4-hour fiction (and its real cost to your schedule) stays intact. The whole world advancing is correct — 4 game-hours of your life genuinely pass (plants grow, time-of-day shifts).

### The scheduling spine (`EmploymentSystem`)

- **Hiring** is a global flag `has_job` (set via dialogue — the intended path, so hire/fire conversations live in `.dlg` files). When `has_job` flips true (listens to `RelationshipSystem.global_flag_changed`), the system schedules the next shift.
- **Scheduling**: next shift 12-36 game-hours out, snapped to a legal start hour. Legal window is **14:00-01:00** (avoids the 02:00-13:59 sleep span; nothing starts after 01:00 since shifts are 4h and the player must get home to sleep). One shift scheduled at a time (player only needs to see their next shift). Shown in the Appointment Panel.
- **Clock-in**: the player walks to the bar's clock-in interactable. It's gated on `can_clock_in()` — a **±15-minute grace window** around the scheduled start (15 early through 15 late). Clocking in launches the minigame via `BarShiftSession.begin_session` (the cross-scene handoff, twin of `StreetDealSession`).
- **Missed shift**: `EmploymentSystem` watches `minute_tick`; if the start + 15 min passes without a clock-in, it's a miss → strike → reschedule (only if not fired).
- **Shift end**: the minigame reports back via a `shift_ended(completed: bool)` signal — `true` = clock ran out (worked it), `false` = clocked out early. Tips are already banked inside the minigame. On end, the spine reschedules the next shift (if still employed). An early clock-out is one strike (same as a no-show — bailing is bailing; a conscious MVP choice).
- **Strikes & firing**: strikes accumulate, reset on `week_rolled`. The strike that pushes the count **past 2** (i.e. the 3rd in a rolling week) fires the player: sets a `fired` global flag, clears `has_job`, notification "Missed shift — you're fired." (Re-hire = clear `fired` + set `has_job` via dialogue, the same flag path — owned by dialogue content, not this system.)
- **Pay**: flat shift wage (~$40, owned by the spine since only it knows attendance) + tips (banked in the minigame). Wage payout wiring lives with the employment layer so a bailed shift doesn't pay a full wage. (Wage is designed; tips are the built earner this session.)

### Architecture notes

- `BarShiftSession` autoload = the `StreetDealSession` twin (cross-scene handoff: store inputs, fade out, swap to mock bar scene; on exit fade back, restore player, emit result).
- The minigame controller adds itself to group `bar_shift`; stations and customers resolve it via `get_tree().get_first_node_in_group("bar_shift")` *lazily on interact* (not cached in `_ready` — the controller is the scene root, so its `_ready` runs after the children's, and a cached lookup would be null).
- Drink ids are clean tokens (`beer`), display names mapped separately (`"Beer"`) — never put prompt/display text in the id, or customer-order matching (`held_drink() == order`) silently never matches.

### Open knobs / remaining polish

- All numbers need tuning: customer patience, per-drink prep times, `SHIFT_TIME_SCALE`, tip ceiling, wage, shift length (4h "might feel a tad long" — time-accel is the relief).
- Bar layout/visual polish; order-icon art (wired, needs final art).
- Strike-system edge testing (week-rollover reset, full-shift completion not striking).
- Optional: hide the clock-in prompt entirely outside the grace window (currently press-and-reject with a notification).
- Wage payout final wiring; re-hire dialogue.

