## 11.7. Scripted Events ✅ STAGE 11 COMPLETE

Authored cutscene system. Designed around editor-driven authoring — events are .tscn scenes, steps are nodes, beat timing is dragged into place visually.

### The "event is a room" model

An event scene is structurally a duplicated room (e.g., duplicate `hardware_store.tscn`, rename to `oliver_intro.tscn` in `res://scenes/events/`). Doorway interactables are stripped manually post-duplication so the player can't escape mid-cutscene.

**Firing = changing rooms.** When an event's conditions pass, EventDirector calls `RoomManager.change_room(event_scene_path, "default")` under cover of a ScreenFade. The active scene becomes the event scene. On completion, change back to the originating room at the player's pre-event position.

**Player handling.** The real Player node is set to `PROCESS_MODE_DISABLED` for the event's duration. Any on-screen player presence during a cutscene is a CutsceneActor puppet.

### Step nodes

All inherit `EventStep extends Node`. Director iterates `Steps.get_children()` and `await`s each `run(event_root)`.

Shipped step types: DialogueStep, ChoiceStep (with `Branch` children), WaitStep, MoveStep, FaceStep, AnimationStep, SetFlagStep, MarkEventDoneStep.

### CutsceneActor

Puppet sprite, not a real NPC. Wraps an AnimatedSprite2D. Carries `dialogue_npc_id`, `display_name`, `default_portrait` for DialogueStep header lookup. Does not interact with NPCDirector, schedules, or any other NPC plumbing.

### Trigger model

Each Event node has:
- `id: String` — globally unique
- `conditions: Array[String]` — precondition strings
- `repeatable: bool` (default false)
- `return_room_path` / `return_spawn` — optional overrides

EventDirector evaluates triggers on `TimeSystem.minute_tick` and `RoomManager.room_changed` (deferred one frame). First eligible event wins. One event runs at a time.

### Condition vocabulary

- `flag` / `!flag` — global flag presence/absence
- `event_done <id>` — RelationshipSystem flag check
- `npc_in_scene <npc_id>` — NPCDirector reports the NPC's current schedule entry is in the current room
- `has_item <item_id>` — stubbed for future use
- `<stat> OP <value>` — compare. Stats: `day`, `dow`, `hour`, `room` (basename). Operators: `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`, `==`, `!=`

Per-NPC affinity/trust intentionally left out of event conditions — set a flag from a dialogue effect or SetFlagStep and gate on the flag.

### Integration with future systems

The flag-then-event pattern is the standard for any system that wants to fire a cutscene from an arbitrary world action: set a flag on success, author an event gated on the flag plus location/time conditions, cutscene fires the next time the player is in the right place. Used for: perfect gift unlocks; intended for: Hank-debt-paid moment (see §14 opening arc), quest turn-ins, detective knock, buyer relationship milestones, criminal-experience tier crossings.

