## 12. Cast

### Thematic frame

The named cast is structured around three age strata × three positions on the same neighborhood decline:

| | Exploit | Endure | Fight / Other response |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Older** (45+) | Hank | Oliver | Rosa (fight), Henry (opt-out) |
| **Middle** (25-45) | Felix | Bruce | Reggie |
| **Younger** (≤25) | — | Erik | Tyrone |

The player is invited into Hank's position by the inciting incident and watches everyone else react.

### Erik & Tyrone — the mirror pair

Two seventeen-year-olds, same neighborhood, different distance down the same road. Erik is where Tyrone might end up; Tyrone is who Erik was three years ago. If the player can affect Tyrone's trajectory — and Rosa's affinity tracks it — there's a moral throughline that emerges from existing systems (relationships, gift-giving, customer roster) rather than scripted quests.

**The central moral hinge of the opening arc:** Tyrone showing up as a potential mark at the school-corner dealing spot. Selling to him sets a flag. Rosa knows (relationship-system flag visible in her dialogue). The game does not punish the player mechanically. It watches.

### Named cast

**Erik** — 17. Homeless. Lives in the camp NW of the apartment building. Positive demeanor despite circumstances, but always either very high or actively trying to get there. Player's likely first NPC encounter (Day 1, on the walk south from the apartment); his conversation is the gateway to Hank ("you wanna make some quick money? Hank's place, back past the camp").

**Henry** — 62. Homeless. Lives in the camp, very close to Erik (they're good friends). Same neighborhood for decades. Knew Hank in younger days; declined to take Hank's path despite ending up worse off, which is the moral content of his character: he opts out rather than exploits. Henry's casual mention of "be careful with that man" lands as character, not warning popup, the first time the player visits Hank.

**Hank** — 50. Built his own shack just past the homeless camp; not technically homeless but lives in self-built marginal housing. Loose moral code on money. Buys stolen goods; sells slim jims, electronics kits, eventually chop-shop access; fronts product to people he reads as desperate enough. The player's criminal-track contact for the entire game. Recurring presence — not just inciting incident, but wholesaler, criminal-job dispatcher, and a study in someone who made the player's potential choices forty years earlier.

**Oliver** — 66. Runs the hardware store. Lived in the neighborhood all his life in the same house, which is now in a very rundown section. Still positive, loves his work, believes there's good in everyone. The "endure with grace" position. Vendor for grow supplies. Already authored end-to-end as the proof-of-concept scheduled NPC.

**Felix** — 30. Runs the pawn shop. Thinks he's very cool — slicked hair, sunglasses, cheesy one-liners, bad jokes. Self-image of the legit operator policing the line between him and Hank ("I don't deal in *that*, my guy"). Vendor for equipment (pager, scale, skateboard, used tools). His stock curation is the primary lever for staging player progression.

**Bruce** — body builder. Hangs out at the gym. Says stuff like "hey, pencil neck." Gameplay function: gym membership gives the player access to targeted skill training (Athletics, Strength). Bruce as a character has more layers than the function suggests — to be developed during cast authoring. Possible angles include ex-boxer worried about the player's trajectory (parallel to Reggie / Hank — yet another middle-aged man with a position on the neighborhood), or someone whose physical confidence masks total non-engagement with the world's actual problems. Decision deferred to dialogue authoring.

**Rosa** — 45. Black, overweight. Elementary school teacher at the school in the NE quadrant. Very sweet but troubled about the direction of the neighborhood. Mother of Tyrone. Her relationship affinity tracks what the player does with respect to her son — fold this into the existing flag system, no new mechanics needed.

**Tyrone** — 17. Rosa's son. Smart, talented, falling in with a bad crowd. Critical that he's authored as a person with his own arc, not just "Rosa's son who might be saved." He can show up at the school-corner dealing spot at certain hours; player's decision whether to sell to him is the moral hinge of the opening arc and shapes Rosa's dialogue going forward.

**Reggie** — beat cop. Good cop, good man. Patrol route covers the central cross of the neighborhood. Wants the player to stay out of trouble. The friend-becomes-disappointed arc requires early-game charm before any "I can't help you anymore" beat can land. Reggie's early dialogue should be warmth-and-coffee, not function-as-cop — if the player only ever talks to him when busted, the betrayal never registers. The dynamic where the player feels increasingly like they're betraying him as they become better friends is the load-bearing emotional arc.

**The neighbor** — sex worker, single mother, lives next door to the player on the same floor. Has a seven-year-old daughter. Name TBD. This character carries the project's "treated seriously" tonal commitment and the design requires more care than the others. Detailed in §12.5 below.

**The neighbor's daughter** — 7. Lives with the neighbor. A person, not a stake. Not endangered, not used as motivation, not a sympathy lever. The player has hallway encounters with her; she may like the player or not. Avoiding the "child in danger" trap is non-negotiable — that frame is itself the violation of the design's intent.

### MVP authoring priorities

The MVP launches with full dialogue support for the characters involved in the opening arc and the core systems:

**Tier 1 (full dialogue + schedules):** Erik, Hank, Oliver, Felix, Reggie, the neighbor
**Tier 2 (full dialogue, smaller schedule):** Rosa, Tyrone
**Tier 3 (lighter dialogue, fixed presence):** Henry, Bruce, the daughter

Tier-3 characters are in the world from MVP launch with minimal but specific dialogue. Their depth fills in post-MVP. This is dramatically more cast than the previous "6 characters" doc target — the difference reflects that the opening arc and tonal commitments need this many real presences in the neighborhood.

