## 13. Locations / Neighborhood Geometry

### One playable neighborhood, one map

The MVP neighborhood is currently authored as a single outdoor scene with four functional quadrants. Apartment interiors, hardware store interior, pawn shop interior, etc. are separate rooms accessed via Doorway transitions.

### Quadrant layout

The map is bisected by a cross of major roads — horizontal east-west and vertical north-south. Four quadrants:

- **NW — The marginal corner.** Hank's shack tucked into the far NW, dark and isolated. Homeless camp (tents + campfire) just south of Hank's. Player's apartment building south of the camp, just north of the road. **The player walks south past the camp every time they leave home.** This makes the Erik-as-gateway-to-Hank beat geographically inevitable — no scripted discovery needed.

- **NE — The civic / family corner.** The school building (Rosa's workplace, where Tyrone attends), basketball court out front. Some industrial / warehouse structure on the east edge — placeholder for late-game expansion (possible chop shop site).

- **SW — The commercial strip.** Pawn shop (Felix), bodega, gas station counter, the strip's parking lot. Oliver's hardware store is *isolated* from the main strip on its own to the west — Oliver was there before the strip grew up.

- **SE — The other margin.** Gas station, then a trailer park / mobile home cluster off to the southeast. The trailer park is the diagonal rhyme of the homeless camp — poverty in different form. Possible home for some recurring street-deal customers (sprite faces the player starts recognizing without ever talking to). Bruce may live here.

### The intersection

The center cross is the single highest-foot-traffic point. School-to-commercial-strip traffic, apartment-to-anywhere traffic, Reggie's beat — they all converge here. Worth thinking of the intersection itself as a kind of stage.

### Reggie's beat

Natural beat-cop loop: perimeter of the central cross. East on the south sidewalk, north on the east side, west on the north sidewalk, south on the west side. He passes every quadrant once per loop. **Critically, he does not enter the NW quadrant.** He walks the road bordering the camp but doesn't cross into Hank's territory unless something brings him in. That spatial separation between the criminal underworld and the cop is the whole game in geometric form.

### Dealing spot placements

Three spots, three stakes (per §7.5):

1. **Gas station spot (SE quadrant)** — Tutorial spot. Mid traffic. Most passersby are straights going to and from the station, low narc rate. The spot Hank points the player to. Always viable, low ceiling.
2. **Pawn alley (SW, behind the strip)** — Time-gated. Daytime: low traffic, low margin. Late night: back-alley crowd, high margin, very low narc rate. Player discovers this on their own.
3. **School corner (NE, near the basketball court)** — Highest after-school traffic. Highest narc density (parents, teachers, Rosa walking home). **Tyrone shows up here.** The moral-weight spot. Game does not point the player here.

### Where characters live

- **Player:** north of the central road, NW quadrant, second floor of the apartment building (or whichever floor — TBD)
- **The neighbor + daughter:** same hallway as player, next door
- **Hank:** NW shack
- **Erik:** SW-most tent in the homeless camp (closest to player's footpath)
- **Henry:** also in the camp, near Erik
- **Oliver:** has his own house (a separate room scene) accessible from the central street, near his hardware store. Per existing schedule authoring.
- **Felix:** likely above or behind his pawn shop, exact home TBD
- **Bruce:** possibly the trailer park (SE)
- **Rosa & Tyrone:** house TBD, somewhere walkable from the school
- **Reggie:** lives outside the neighborhood (he commutes in for his beat) — his home doesn't need to be in the playable map

### Outdoor chunking (future)

Currently one outdoor scene. As the map expands post-MVP, the chunking framework (§22) splits into 4-7 outdoor scenes with `Doorway`-style transitions between chunks. The current single-scene approach is the MVP target.

