## 25. Post-MVP Design Threads (captured, not built)

Substantial design discussion that isn't implemented but is load-bearing for the game's identity. Captured here so a future instance has the reasoning, not just the feature names. None of this is in MVP scope.

### 25.1 Two-meter trouble model: Heat vs. Scrutiny

The current "personal heat" (§15.2) is underdefined. The intended resolution is **two distinct meters**, because they answer different player questions and are reduced by different actions:

- **Heat** — "did someone see you commit a crime." The witnessed-crime axis (witness model, pursuit, area heat) already partly exists (§15). Reduced by laying low, not being seen. Feeds the courthouse (§25.2).
- **Scrutiny / suspicion** — "does your money make sense." A *financial* pressure, separate from police heat. Reduced by laundering / spending less conspicuously / having clean income. Feeds the clean-cash gates (§25.3).

Keep them separate even though both are "you're in trouble" states — conflating them muddies what the player should *do* about each. Court is "stop getting seen"; scrutiny is "clean up your money."

### 25.2 Courthouse / Adjudication System

When personal **heat** crosses a threshold, the player gets a **court date**. At court, a record of all witnessed crimes is tallied (this implies witnessed crimes need to **persist as a permanent ledger** — cleared at adjudication — in addition to feeding the decaying heat meter; design heat as "decaying meter + permanent rap sheet" from the start). Punishment ladder, all denominated in the game's true currency — **lost time/attention**, not just money:

- **Fine** — near-zero time cost, just cash.
- **Community service** — scheduled time-loss. Rides the *same scheduling spine as bartending* (§7.9) — a forced scheduled obligation that locks out your other activities. This is a direct reuse of the employment/scheduling system.
- **Jail** — catastrophic block-time-loss. Several days gone. The cascade is the punishment: during jail, pages keep coming in and you flake on all of them (trust loss across the roster), and plants die from not being watered. You don't need bespoke jail pain — it borrows pain from every system that runs on the schedule clock.

Design principle that makes this good: it's punishment in *time*, not money, so it cascades through plants/customers/jobs automatically. Tuned to be "weather you plan around," not a wall — this is a supplement-to-crime game, crime should stay the fun part, so the system shouldn't make players crime-averse. No game-over; consequences are state shifts (consistent with §15.5).

### 25.3 Clean vs. Dirty Cash

Architected via the multi-pool wallet (§5) but inert for MVP. The intended model:

- Cash carries a **clean/dirty** tag. Most income (dealing, crime) is dirty.
- **Dirty cash spends freely in the *informal* economy** — pawn shop (Felix), Hank, gas station, hardware store (Oliver). No friction on everyday spend. This is essential: the mechanic is only fun if dirty money is usefully spendable, just *restricted*, not a tax on every purchase.
- **Clean cash is required only at *formal* gates** — rent, any property purchase/upgrade (the housing ladder §23.5), and **mail-order equipment** (planter beds, grow lights, mylar, furniture — the catalogue). Putting the grow op's *own scaling equipment* behind clean cash couples production growth to laundering: you can deal dirty all day, but you can't *expand the farm* without cleaning up. That coupling is the intended pressure.
- The gate is the fun ("I can afford the $3,000 room but only in dirty cash, and that raises suspicion"); friction-on-everything is the failure mode to avoid.

This is the thing that makes the legit jobs (§7.9, §25.4) matter long-term: a bar shift's $80 is laughable next to a tier-4 deal, but if it's *clean* it's not competing with the deal — it's enabling you to *spend* the deal money. Legit work becomes a laundering/access mechanism, not a parallel income track that has to be balanced against drug profit. That's why the legit axis is a **supplement**, not an alternative win condition.

**Open question that gates the whole thing:** the laundering *rate*. How much dirty cash does a session generate, and therefore how fast must clean cash flow to make laundering a paced decision rather than a grind or a rubber-stamp? This needs playtest instrumentation on the (post-overhaul) economy before clean/dirty can be tuned. Don't build clean/dirty into a fresh, un-stabilized economy.

### 25.4 The rest of the legit axis

- **Delivery job** (planned) — pickup→dropoff on a timer, reuses meetspots/inventory/timers. Held *behind map work*: it's too similar to dealing's verb until the map has new areas with vaultable fences/shortcuts that make it a *spatial routing* puzzle. Building it now would lock in the boring version.
- **Panhandling** (planned) — a low-risk reskin of the street-deal loop, no product, less money; heat/affinity loss if real NPCs walk by. Held *behind ambient-NPC work* — it's a ~1-day feature once that substrate exists, a 3-day feature if built just for it. The day-one desperation valve, not a career.
- **Fronts / owned businesses** (planned) — the eventual clean-cash *firehose* and laundering endgame. Lemonade stand as the comedic first front (works as a plain small-income front pre-laundering, becomes funnier post-laundering — "a lemonade stand clearing $4k/week"), preferred over a laundromat (too on-the-nose, depends on clean/dirty existing to make sense). Generalizes the apartment-complex "owned thing that pays weekly" pattern (§23.5). This is where the legit and criminal paths converge — a front is both.

**Sequencing intent:** legit jobs are the early-game clean trickle that bootstraps you toward affording a front; fronts are the late-game clean firehose. Legit stays a supplement throughout — no parallel tier ladder, no balancing legit income against drug income, because the value is laundering and access, not raw profit.

